;;; How `sdql.egg` corresponds to the reference crate.
;;;
;;; The language file says what the constructors ARE; this says what the reference
;;; calls them, which is the only thing a differential harness needs that the encoding
;;; itself does not. Keeping it out of the .egg file keeps the .egg file a language
;;; declaration rather than a harness configuration.
;;;
;;; One line per operator:
;;;
;;;     <operator>  <Constructor>  <reference>
;;;
;;; `<operator>` is the name terms and rules are written with. `<reference>` is the tag
;;; the reference's parser uses, or `=payload` where the reference writes the payload
;;; itself instead of a tag -- optionally `=payload <prefix>` where it needs one.
;;;
;;; Two entries are not just a rename, and both are the reference working around
;;; itself rather than anything about the encoding:
;;;
;;;   let    the oracle holds two languages in one `define_language!`, so `let` is
;;;          already taken by the array language's differently-shaped `let`
;;;   sym    the oracle matches operator tags before payloads, so a bare symbol in a
;;;          child position parses as an operator unless it is prefixed

lambda      Lambda      lambda
sing        Sing        sing
add         Add         +
mult        Mult        *
sub         Sub         -
eq          Equality    eq
get         Get         get
range       Range       range
apply       App         apply
ifthen      IfThen      ifthen
binop       Binop       binop
subarray    SubArray    subarray
unique      Unique      unique
sum         Sum         sum
merge       Merge       merge
let         Let         sdql-let
num         Num         =payload
sym         Symbol      =payload sym:
null        Null        null
